1
00:00:08,460 --> 00:00:11,900
But noble Odysseus made his bed in the fore-hall:

2
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:18,900
he spread an undressed oxhide down, and over it many
fleeces of the sheep the Achaeans kept slaughtering,

3
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:22,100
and Eurynome threw a mantle over him where he lay.

4
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:28,880
There Odysseus lay awake, his heart devising evils
for the suitors; and out of the hall came the women,

5
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:32,260
the ones who all along had been sleeping with the suitors,

6
00:00:32,260 --> 00:00:35,620
setting each other laughing, trading their good cheer.

7
00:00:35,620 --> 00:00:40,960
And the heart was roused in his breast,
and he weighed it long in his mind and in his heart —

8
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:46,360
whether to spring after them and deal out death to each,
or let them mix with the overweening suitors

9
00:00:46,360 --> 00:00:53,440
one final and last time. And the heart inside him snarled —
as a bitch standing over her soft-boned pups,

10
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:57,360
facing a man she does not know,
snarls and is wild to fight:

11
00:00:57,360 --> 00:01:01,780
so his heart snarled within him,
in outrage at their evil doings.

12
00:01:01,780 --> 00:01:04,760
But he struck his chest and called his heart to order:

13
00:01:04,760 --> 00:01:08,660
"Bear up, my heart.
A more dog-vile thing you bore than this

14
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:14,380
the day the Cyclops, his force past holding,
ate my brave companions — and you endured it, till cunning

15
00:01:14,380 --> 00:01:17,280
led you out of the cave where you thought to die."

16
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:23,220
So he spoke, reproving the dear heart in his breast;
and his heart, in deep obedience, held fast

17
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:28,140
and endured without a break. But the
man himself turned this way and that.

18
00:01:28,140 --> 00:01:33,000
As when a man before a great blazing fire
turns a paunch packed full of fat and blood

19
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:38,820
this way and that, longing to have it roasted quickly,
so he rolled from side to side, turning over

20
00:01:38,820 --> 00:01:45,460
how he could lay his hands on the shameless suitors,
one man against so many. And Athena came close to him,

21
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:51,000
down from the sky; and in body she was like a woman;
and she stood above his head and spoke to him:

22
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,720
"Why awake again, most luckless of all men?

23
00:01:53,720 --> 00:01:59,880
Here is your house; here in the house is your wife,
and your child — a son such as any man would pray for."

24
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:02,580
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

25
00:02:02,580 --> 00:02:05,800
"Yes, goddess — all this you have said as it should be.

26
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:08,280
But there is a thing my heart keeps turning over:

27
00:02:08,280 --> 00:02:14,540
how I am to lay hands on the shameless suitors — I,
one man alone; and they always inside in a crowd.

28
00:02:14,540 --> 00:02:17,380
And this greater thing besides I keep turning over:

29
00:02:17,380 --> 00:02:20,800
even if I kill them, by the will of Zeus and by yours,

30
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:24,760
where do I run from what follows?
Think that out for me, I say."

31
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:27,600
Then the goddess answered him, gray-eyed Athena:

32
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:33,760
"Hard man! A person will trust a weaker friend than me,
one who is mortal, and does not know such counsels;

33
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:38,780
but I am a god, and I guard you through to the
end of every labor. I will tell you outright:

34
00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:43,640
if fifty companies of mortal men stood round
the two of us, hot to kill us in the fight,

35
00:02:43,640 --> 00:02:47,440
even so you would drive away their cattle and fat sheep.

36
00:02:47,440 --> 00:02:50,400
Come — let sleep take you too. There is misery in watching

37
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:53,600
all night awake. You will rise up out of your evils now."

38
00:02:53,600 --> 00:03:00,280
So she spoke, and poured down sleep upon his eyelids,
and went back herself to Olympus, the shining goddess.

39
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:07,120
And as sleep seized him, loosing the cares of his heart,
loosing his limbs, his true-hearted wife awoke,

40
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:09,840
and wept, sitting up on her soft bed.

41
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:15,900
And when she had had her fill of weeping,
first to Artemis prayed that woman shining among women:

42
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:19,440
"Artemis, sovereign goddess,
daughter of Zeus — would that now

43
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:25,560
you would plant an arrow in my breast and take my
life this very instant — or a storm-wind snatch me up

44
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:31,340
and carry me off down the ways of gloom and fling
me down where backward-flowing Ocean empties.

45
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:34,560
As when the storm-winds carried off Pandareus' daughters:

46
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:37,220
the gods had destroyed their parents, and they were left

47
00:03:37,220 --> 00:03:43,220
orphans in the halls; and shining Aphrodite reared
them on cheese and sweet honey and pleasant wine,

48
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:48,360
and Hera gave them beauty and good sense beyond
all women, and pure Artemis their height,

49
00:03:48,360 --> 00:03:50,940
and Athena taught them the making of glorious work.

50
00:03:50,940 --> 00:03:54,120
But while shining Aphrodite was on her way to high Olympus

51
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:58,060
to ask for the girls the crown of blossoming marriage —

52
00:03:58,060 --> 00:04:02,200
gone to Zeus who delights in thunder,
for he knows all things well,

53
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:08,060
the fortune and the misfortune of mortal men —
meanwhile the snatching-winds carried the girls away

54
00:04:08,060 --> 00:04:11,860
and gave them over to the hateful Furies, to serve them.

55
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:18,060
So may the dwellers on Olympus blot me out of sight,
or fair-braided Artemis strike me, that with Odysseus

56
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:24,740
before my eyes I may pass even under the hateful earth,
and never gladden the mind of a lesser man.

57
00:04:24,740 --> 00:04:30,640
And yet an evil is bearable, when a person weeps through
the days, grieved to the packed core of the heart,

58
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:33,880
and the nights hold sleep at least —
for sleep makes one forget

59
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:38,380
everything, the good and the bad,
once it has wrapped the eyelids —

60
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:41,860
but to me the god sends evil even in dreams.

61
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:45,400
For this very night one slept beside me — like him,

62
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:48,760
his very likeness as he was when
he went with the army; and my heart

63
00:04:48,760 --> 00:04:53,180
rejoiced: I thought it no dream,
but the waking truth at last."

64
00:04:53,180 --> 00:04:57,280
So she spoke; and at once Dawn
of the golden throne came on.

65
00:04:57,280 --> 00:05:00,120
And noble Odysseus caught the sound of her weeping,

66
00:05:00,120 --> 00:05:03,260
and mused; and it seemed to him,
in the drift of his heart,

67
00:05:03,260 --> 00:05:06,980
that she knew him already, and stood beside his head.

68
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:12,700
He gathered up the mantle and the fleeces he had slept
in and set them on a chair in the hall; the oxhide

69
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:17,420
he carried outside and laid down;
and lifting his hands he prayed to Zeus:

70
00:05:17,420 --> 00:05:20,440
"Father Zeus — if you gods willed it, and brought me home

71
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:26,420
over dry land and water, after all your hard usage,
let someone of the people waking in the house speak me

72
00:05:26,420 --> 00:05:30,860
a word of omen; and outside,
let another sign of Zeus appear."

73
00:05:30,860 --> 00:05:36,600
So he prayed; and Zeus the counselor heard him,
and at once he thundered out of gleaming Olympus,

74
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:41,080
down from on high, out of the clouds.
And noble Odysseus was glad.

75
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:44,260
And the word of omen came from
a woman grinding at the mill,

76
00:05:44,260 --> 00:05:47,480
close by, where the mills of the
shepherd of the people stood.

77
00:05:47,480 --> 00:05:53,260
Twelve women in all kept them turning,
making barley meal and wheat flour, the marrow of men.

78
00:05:53,260 --> 00:05:58,940
The others were asleep, their wheat all ground;
only one had not finished — the frailest one of all.

79
00:05:58,940 --> 00:06:02,800
She stopped her stone now,
and spoke the word — a sign for her master:

80
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:05,740
"Father Zeus, you who rule the gods and mankind:

81
00:06:05,740 --> 00:06:11,980
how loud you thundered out of the starry sky —
and no cloud anywhere! You show this as a sign to someone.

82
00:06:11,980 --> 00:06:15,300
Then accomplish now for wretched me the word I say:

83
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:19,020
may the suitors, on this day,
for the last and latest time,

84
00:06:19,020 --> 00:06:21,920
take up their lovely feasting in the halls of Odysseus —

85
00:06:21,920 --> 00:06:24,980
the men who have loosed my knees with heart-grinding labor

86
00:06:24,980 --> 00:06:28,760
as I make their barley meal: may they sup now their last!"

87
00:06:28,760 --> 00:06:32,900
So she spoke; and noble Odysseus
rejoiced at the omen of her word

88
00:06:32,900 --> 00:06:36,880
and at the thunder of Zeus.
He told himself the guilty would pay.

89
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:40,760
Now the other slave-women, gathering
in the beautiful house of Odysseus,

90
00:06:40,760 --> 00:06:44,760
were kindling on the hearth the never-wearying fire.

91
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:48,200
And Telemachus rose from his bed, a man like a god,

92
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:51,600
and put on his clothes, and hung
his sharp sword at his shoulder,

93
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:55,120
and under his shining feet he bound the beautiful sandals,

94
00:06:55,120 --> 00:07:01,840
and took his strong spear, tipped with sharpened bronze,
and paused on the threshold, and said to Eurycleia:

95
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:04,540
"Dear nurse, have you honored the stranger in our house

96
00:07:04,540 --> 00:07:08,480
with bed and food? Or does he lie untended, as he may?

97
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:11,580
That is my mother's way — sensible woman though she is:

98
00:07:11,580 --> 00:07:17,700
she honors the worse of mortal men on impulse,
and sends the better man away unhonored."

99
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:20,260
Then watchful-minded Eurycleia answered him:

100
00:07:20,260 --> 00:07:23,080
"Child, do not blame the blameless woman now.

101
00:07:23,080 --> 00:07:26,360
He sat and drank his wine as long as he himself would;

102
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:29,860
and bread — he said he hungered for none: she asked him.

103
00:07:29,860 --> 00:07:34,860
And when he began to think of rest and sleep,
she told the slave-women to spread him a proper bed;

104
00:07:34,860 --> 00:07:41,140
but he — like a man wholly wretched, out of luck —
refused to sleep on a bedstead under blankets:

105
00:07:41,140 --> 00:07:43,600
on an undressed oxhide and the fleeces of sheep

106
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:47,780
he slept in the fore-hall.
We laid a mantle over him ourselves."

107
00:07:47,780 --> 00:07:54,340
So she spoke; and Telemachus went out through the hall,
spear in hand; two swift dogs followed with him,

108
00:07:54,340 --> 00:07:58,260
and he went to the assembly-place,
to the strong-greaved Achaeans.

109
00:07:58,260 --> 00:08:02,460
And she called out to the slave-women,
that woman shining among women —

110
00:08:02,460 --> 00:08:05,120
Eurycleia, daughter of Ops, Peisenor's son:

111
00:08:05,120 --> 00:08:11,800
"Come — to work! Some of you sweep the house, and briskly,
and sprinkle it, and lay the crimson coverings

112
00:08:11,800 --> 00:08:18,180
on the well-made chairs; others take sponges and
wipe down all the tables, and rinse the mixing-bowls

113
00:08:18,180 --> 00:08:21,280
and the wrought double-handled cups;
and the rest of you away

114
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:25,180
for water at the spring —
go quick, and quick back with it.

115
00:08:25,180 --> 00:08:27,820
The suitors will not be long out of the hall this morning:

116
00:08:27,820 --> 00:08:31,680
they will come very early.
It is festival for everyone today."

117
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:34,820
So she spoke; and they listened well and obeyed:

118
00:08:34,820 --> 00:08:40,140
twenty went off to the spring of dark water,
and the others worked away skillfully about the house.

119
00:08:40,140 --> 00:08:45,880
Then the Achaeans' serving-men came in, and split firewood,
well and skillfully; and the women came back

120
00:08:45,880 --> 00:08:52,260
from the spring; and after them came the swineherd,
driving three fatted hogs, the best of all he had.

121
00:08:52,260 --> 00:08:55,320
He left them to feed at large in the fine yard-fences,

122
00:08:55,320 --> 00:08:58,800
and himself spoke to Odysseus, and his words were kind:

123
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:01,940
"Stranger — do the Achaeans look on you any better now,

124
00:09:01,940 --> 00:09:05,540
or do they still dishonor you
through the hall, as before?"

125
00:09:05,540 --> 00:09:08,340
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

126
00:09:08,340 --> 00:09:13,520
"Ah, Eumaeus, may the gods pay back the outrage
these men in their insolence keep devising —

127
00:09:13,520 --> 00:09:17,820
reckless things, in another man's house.
They have no share of shame."

128
00:09:17,820 --> 00:09:20,120
So they spoke to one another of such things.

129
00:09:20,120 --> 00:09:25,860
And Melanthius came up close to them, the goatherd,
driving the goats that stood out in all the herds,

130
00:09:25,860 --> 00:09:29,180
a dinner for the suitors;
and two herdsmen followed with him.

131
00:09:29,180 --> 00:09:35,540
He tethered the goats under the echoing porch,
and himself spoke to Odysseus, and his words were taunts:

132
00:09:35,540 --> 00:09:41,160
"Stranger — still here? Still going to plague this house,
begging from the men? Still not out the door?

133
00:09:41,160 --> 00:09:43,740
I don't imagine, now, we two will settle this business

134
00:09:43,740 --> 00:09:46,860
before we've tasted each other's
fists. There is no decency

135
00:09:46,860 --> 00:09:50,700
in the way you beg. And there are
other feasts among the Achaeans."

136
00:09:50,700 --> 00:09:54,680
So he spoke; and Odysseus of
the many designs said nothing:

137
00:09:54,680 --> 00:09:58,720
he only shook his head, silent,
brooding evils deep in his heart.

138
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:02,940
And a third came in upon them —
Philoetius, a leader of men,

139
00:10:02,940 --> 00:10:06,340
driving a barren heifer for the suitors, and fat goats.

140
00:10:06,340 --> 00:10:11,600
Ferrymen had brought them over — the ones who
carry other travelers too, whoever comes to them.

141
00:10:11,600 --> 00:10:17,160
He tethered his beasts well under the echoing porch,
and came up close to the swineherd and asked him:

142
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:22,560
"Swineherd — who is this stranger, newly come to
our house? What men does he claim for his people?

143
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:26,000
Where are his family and his fathers' fields?

144
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:30,680
Poor devil — and yet, truly,
in build he is like a lord, a king.

145
00:10:30,680 --> 00:10:36,440
But the gods sink far-wandering men in misery
when they spin hard sorrow even for kings."

146
00:10:36,440 --> 00:10:42,900
He spoke, and coming up he gave him his right hand in
welcome, and lifting his voice he spoke winged words:

147
00:10:42,900 --> 00:10:45,940
"Your health, father stranger! May happiness be yours

148
00:10:45,940 --> 00:10:50,400
in time to come — though now
you are held fast in many evils.

149
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:53,540
Father Zeus — no other god is deadlier than you:

150
00:10:53,540 --> 00:11:00,100
you have no pity on men, though you begot them yourself,
for tangling them in misery and grinding pains.

151
00:11:00,100 --> 00:11:07,400
I broke sweat when I saw him, and my eyes stand full
of tears, remembering Odysseus: for he too, I think,

152
00:11:07,400 --> 00:11:13,980
wears rags like these, and wanders somewhere among men —
if indeed he lives, and sees the light of the sun.

153
00:11:13,980 --> 00:11:17,800
But if he is dead already, down in the house of Hades —

154
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:21,040
ah me, blameless Odysseus! — who set me over his cattle

155
00:11:21,040 --> 00:11:24,900
when I was still a boy, in the land of the Cephallenians.

156
00:11:24,900 --> 00:11:28,020
And now they are past all counting: no other man

157
00:11:28,020 --> 00:11:32,380
could have a breed of broad-browed
cattle bear richer, like standing wheat.

158
00:11:32,380 --> 00:11:37,560
And strangers order me to drive them in for them to eat!
They care nothing for the son in the house,

159
00:11:37,560 --> 00:11:40,680
they do not tremble at the gods'
watching eye: they are wild

160
00:11:40,680 --> 00:11:43,820
to divide up the goods of the master so long away.

161
00:11:43,820 --> 00:11:49,560
As for me, this thought keeps rolling over and over
in the heart in my breast: it is a very bad thing,

162
00:11:49,560 --> 00:11:53,240
while the son lives, to go off to a country of strangers,

163
00:11:53,240 --> 00:11:56,860
cattle and all, to foreign men; but colder still to stay

164
00:11:56,860 --> 00:12:00,200
suffering here, sitting over cattle that others claim.

165
00:12:00,200 --> 00:12:06,280
And long ago I would have run, fled to some other of
the high-handed kings — for this is past all bearing —

166
00:12:06,280 --> 00:12:09,340
but still I think of that unlucky man: what if he came

167
00:12:09,340 --> 00:12:13,460
from somewhere, and made a scattering
of the suitors through the house!"

168
00:12:13,460 --> 00:12:16,240
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

169
00:12:16,240 --> 00:12:19,480
"Cowherd — since you seem no bad man and no fool,

170
00:12:19,480 --> 00:12:22,640
and I can see for myself that
good sense has reached your heart,

171
00:12:22,640 --> 00:12:26,080
I will tell you something, and swear a great oath upon it:

172
00:12:26,080 --> 00:12:30,460
Now Zeus be my witness, first of the gods,
and this table of friendship,

173
00:12:30,460 --> 00:12:33,180
and the hearth of blameless Odysseus,
to which I have come:

174
00:12:33,180 --> 00:12:39,360
while you are here, Odysseus will come home;
and with your own eyes, if you wish it, you shall see

175
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:42,800
the killing of the suitors who play lord and master here."

176
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:45,960
Then the cowherd answered him, the master of the oxen:

177
00:12:45,960 --> 00:12:49,600
"Ah, stranger, if only the son of
Cronos would accomplish that word!

178
00:12:49,600 --> 00:12:52,940
Then you would learn my strength —
and how my hands follow it."

179
00:12:52,940 --> 00:12:55,320
And in the same way Eumaeus prayed to all the gods

180
00:12:55,320 --> 00:12:58,940
that Odysseus of the many designs
might come home to his own house.

181
00:12:58,940 --> 00:13:02,180
So they spoke to one another of such things.

182
00:13:02,180 --> 00:13:05,000
And the suitors, meanwhile, were framing death and doom

183
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:08,540
for Telemachus — when a bird came by on their left hand:

184
00:13:08,540 --> 00:13:11,760
an eagle flying high, gripping a trembling dove.

185
00:13:11,760 --> 00:13:14,240
And Amphinomus rose and spoke among them:

186
00:13:14,240 --> 00:13:20,640
"Friends, this plan of ours will not run smooth —
the killing of Telemachus. Let us think of the feast."

187
00:13:20,640 --> 00:13:23,920
So spoke Amphinomus, and his word pleased them well.

188
00:13:23,920 --> 00:13:29,380
They came into the house of godlike Odysseus and
laid their mantles down on the couches and chairs

189
00:13:29,380 --> 00:13:34,180
and slaughtered big sheep and fat goats,
slaughtered sleek hogs and a heifer of the herd.

190
00:13:34,180 --> 00:13:37,000
They roasted the innards and served them round, and mixed

191
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:41,200
the wine in the mixing-bowls;
and the swineherd passed the cups,

192
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:47,760
and Philoetius, a leader of men, passed them bread
in beautiful baskets; and Melanthius poured the wine.

193
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:51,100
And their hands went out to the
good things spread before them.

194
00:13:51,100 --> 00:13:54,520
But Telemachus — his craft at work — seated Odysseus

195
00:13:54,520 --> 00:14:00,520
inside the strong-built hall, beside the stone threshold,
setting him a battered stool and a little table;

196
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:06,080
he put a portion of the innards by him,
and poured him wine in a golden cup, and said to him:

197
00:14:06,080 --> 00:14:08,900
"Sit here now among the men, and drink your wine.

198
00:14:08,900 --> 00:14:14,600
The taunts and the fists of all the suitors —
I myself will hold them off you. This is no public house:

199
00:14:14,600 --> 00:14:17,640
it is Odysseus' house — and he won it for me.

200
00:14:17,640 --> 00:14:24,060
And you, you suitors, hold your hearts back from insult
and fists, so no strife and quarreling break out."

201
00:14:24,060 --> 00:14:29,820
So he spoke; and they all bit their lips and stared
in wonder at Telemachus, that he spoke so boldly.

202
00:14:29,820 --> 00:14:33,980
And Antinous spoke among them, Eupeithes' son:

203
00:14:33,980 --> 00:14:40,140
"Hard as it is, Achaeans, let us take the word of
Telemachus — though how he threatens as he talks!

204
00:14:40,140 --> 00:14:43,560
Zeus the son of Cronos would not have it; otherwise

205
00:14:43,560 --> 00:14:48,300
we would have silenced him in the
halls long since — so clear a speaker."

206
00:14:48,300 --> 00:14:51,980
So spoke Antinous; but he took no heed of his words.

207
00:14:51,980 --> 00:14:54,760
Now the heralds were leading
through the town the holy hecatomb

208
00:14:54,760 --> 00:15:01,700
of the gods; and the long-haired Achaeans gathered
under the shady grove of Apollo who shoots from afar.

209
00:15:01,700 --> 00:15:04,760
And when they had roasted the
outer flesh and drawn it off,

210
00:15:04,760 --> 00:15:10,640
they divided the portions and feasted the glorious feast;
and the serving-men set beside Odysseus a share

211
00:15:10,640 --> 00:15:17,180
equal to the ones they drew themselves:
for so ordered Telemachus, dear son of godlike Odysseus.

212
00:15:17,180 --> 00:15:23,180
And Athena would not let the lordly suitors hold back
from heart-stinging outrage — so that pain might sink

213
00:15:23,180 --> 00:15:26,800
still deeper into Laertes' son Odysseus.

214
00:15:26,800 --> 00:15:29,980
There was among the suitors a man versed in lawlessness:

215
00:15:29,980 --> 00:15:35,660
Ctesippus was his name, and his home was on Same;
sure of his fabulous wealth, he had come

216
00:15:35,660 --> 00:15:39,520
to woo the wife of Odysseus, the master so long away.

217
00:15:39,520 --> 00:15:42,560
He now spoke out among the overweening suitors:

218
00:15:42,560 --> 00:15:45,520
"Hear me, you lordly suitors, while I say a word.

219
00:15:45,520 --> 00:15:48,600
The stranger has long had his portion — an equal one,

220
00:15:48,600 --> 00:15:52,660
as is proper: it is not fine, and it is not just, to stint

221
00:15:52,660 --> 00:15:55,980
the guests of Telemachus, whoever comes to this house.

222
00:15:55,980 --> 00:15:59,520
But come — let me too give him a guest-gift, so he in turn

223
00:15:59,520 --> 00:16:05,260
can hand a prize to the bath-woman, or to some other
of the slaves in the house of godlike Odysseus."

224
00:16:05,260 --> 00:16:07,960
So speaking, he snatched an ox-hoof out of the basket

225
00:16:07,960 --> 00:16:11,800
where it lay, and hurled it with
his thick hand. But Odysseus

226
00:16:11,800 --> 00:16:15,100
dodged it with a quick tilt of
his head, and smiled in his heart

227
00:16:15,100 --> 00:16:19,280
a smile most sardonic; and it struck the well-built wall.

228
00:16:19,280 --> 00:16:22,520
And Telemachus lashed Ctesippus with his words:

229
00:16:22,520 --> 00:16:25,600
"Ctesippus — count it clear profit for your own heart:

230
00:16:25,600 --> 00:16:29,040
you missed the stranger. He dodged the throw himself.

231
00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:32,280
Otherwise I would have run you through
the middle with my sharp spear,

232
00:16:32,280 --> 00:16:37,940
and instead of a wedding your father would be busy
here with a funeral. So: let no man show his ugliness

233
00:16:37,940 --> 00:16:41,920
in this house of mine. For now I
take note and I know each thing,

234
00:16:41,920 --> 00:16:45,300
the good and the worse alike. Before, I was still a child.

235
00:16:45,300 --> 00:16:47,720
But even so we endure it and look on:

236
00:16:47,720 --> 00:16:53,520
the flocks slaughtered, the wine drunk down,
the bread — for it is hard for one man to check many.

237
00:16:53,520 --> 00:16:55,920
Come then, do me no more evil in your malice.

238
00:16:55,920 --> 00:16:59,620
But if you are set on killing me —
me myself — with the bronze,

239
00:16:59,620 --> 00:17:05,640
even that I would choose: it would be far better to
die than look on forever at these disgraceful doings:

240
00:17:05,640 --> 00:17:09,980
guests roughly handled, and men hauling the
slave-women shamefully through the beautiful house."

241
00:17:09,980 --> 00:17:13,820
So he spoke; and they all fell silent and still.

242
00:17:13,820 --> 00:17:17,180
At last Agelaus spoke, Damastor's son:

243
00:17:17,180 --> 00:17:22,960
"Friends — no man should take offense at a just word
spoken, and answer it with wrangling and hard speech.

244
00:17:22,960 --> 00:17:29,100
Do not knock the stranger about, nor any other
of the slaves in the house of godlike Odysseus.

245
00:17:29,100 --> 00:17:31,800
But to Telemachus, and to his mother, I would say

246
00:17:31,800 --> 00:17:35,500
a gentle word — if it might find
favor with the hearts of both.

247
00:17:35,500 --> 00:17:38,020
As long as the hearts in your breasts kept hope

248
00:17:38,020 --> 00:17:42,000
that Odysseus of the many designs
would come home to his own house,

249
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:47,620
no one could resent your waiting, or your holding
off the suitors in the halls: that was the better way

250
00:17:47,620 --> 00:17:51,360
if Odysseus had returned, come back at last to his house.

251
00:17:51,360 --> 00:17:54,100
But now it is plain that he is past returning.

252
00:17:54,100 --> 00:17:57,140
Go then, sit by your mother, and lay it out for her:

253
00:17:57,140 --> 00:18:02,640
marry the best man, the one who offers most —
so you may keep and enjoy all that was your father's,

254
00:18:02,640 --> 00:18:05,940
eating and drinking, and she may keep another's house."

255
00:18:05,940 --> 00:18:08,280
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

256
00:18:08,280 --> 00:18:13,300
"No, by Zeus, Agelaus —
and by the sufferings of my father,

257
00:18:13,300 --> 00:18:16,220
who far from Ithaca has perished, or still wanders:

258
00:18:16,220 --> 00:18:22,960
I do not delay my mother's marriage. I urge her to wed
the man she chooses; and I offer gifts past counting.

259
00:18:22,960 --> 00:18:25,600
But I am ashamed to drive her
from the hall against her will

260
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:29,960
with a word of compulsion.
May god never bring that to pass."

261
00:18:29,960 --> 00:18:33,380
So spoke Telemachus. And in the suitors Pallas Athena

262
00:18:33,380 --> 00:18:37,680
set off unquenchable laughter,
and knocked their wits askew.

263
00:18:37,680 --> 00:18:40,840
And now they were laughing with jaws no longer their own;

264
00:18:40,840 --> 00:18:43,740
the meat they ate was dabbled in blood; and their eyes

265
00:18:43,740 --> 00:18:47,760
brimmed full of tears;
and the heart in them wanted to wail.

266
00:18:47,760 --> 00:18:50,660
And godlike Theoclymenus spoke out among them:

267
00:18:50,660 --> 00:18:54,400
"Wretched men — what evil is this
you suffer? Night — your heads

268
00:18:54,400 --> 00:18:58,440
are shrouded in night, and your
faces, and your knees beneath;

269
00:18:58,440 --> 00:19:01,700
wailing has caught like fire; cheeks are streaming tears;

270
00:19:01,700 --> 00:19:05,620
the walls are spattered with blood,
and the fine crossbeams;

271
00:19:05,620 --> 00:19:09,340
and the porch is full of ghosts —
the courtyard full of them —

272
00:19:09,340 --> 00:19:12,580
ghosts hurrying down to Erebus, under the gloom; the sun

273
00:19:12,580 --> 00:19:17,020
has perished out of the sky,
and an evil mist runs over everything."

274
00:19:17,020 --> 00:19:20,020
So he spoke; and they all laughed at him sweetly.

275
00:19:20,020 --> 00:19:23,760
And Eurymachus, Polybus' son, began to speak among them:

276
00:19:23,760 --> 00:19:27,240
"The stranger is raving — this new one, fresh from abroad.

277
00:19:27,240 --> 00:19:30,220
Quick, young men — see him out of the house and on his way

278
00:19:30,220 --> 00:19:33,740
to the assembly-place, since he finds it night in here."

279
00:19:33,740 --> 00:19:36,300
Then godlike Theoclymenus said to him:

280
00:19:36,300 --> 00:19:39,500
"Eurymachus, I ask no escort of you for my going.

281
00:19:39,500 --> 00:19:44,840
I have eyes, and ears, and both my feet,
and a mind in my breast in no way meanly made.

282
00:19:44,840 --> 00:19:51,140
With these I am going out that door — for I see evil
coming on you, evil no man will escape or dodge,

283
00:19:51,140 --> 00:19:54,500
no man of you suitors, you who
in the house of godlike Odysseus

284
00:19:54,500 --> 00:19:58,120
batter men with outrage and devise your reckless deeds."

285
00:19:58,120 --> 00:20:03,580
So speaking he went out of the well-settled house
and came to Piraeus, who received him gladly.

286
00:20:03,580 --> 00:20:09,300
And the suitors all, eyeing one another, went to
work needling Telemachus, laughing at his guests;

287
00:20:09,300 --> 00:20:11,900
and a man of the arrogant young men would say:

288
00:20:11,900 --> 00:20:15,380
"Telemachus, no man is unluckier in his guests than you.

289
00:20:15,380 --> 00:20:20,980
Look at this one you keep — this pawed-over vagrant
who wants his bread and wine, who has no skill for work

290
00:20:20,980 --> 00:20:24,220
and no strength either: a dead weight on the good earth.

291
00:20:24,220 --> 00:20:26,420
And then the other one — he stood up to play the prophet!

292
00:20:26,420 --> 00:20:29,600
If you would listen to me, the profit is all yours:

293
00:20:29,600 --> 00:20:31,900
let us pitch your guests into a many-benched ship

294
00:20:31,900 --> 00:20:36,320
and send them to the Sicilians.
There they would fetch you a worthy price."

295
00:20:36,320 --> 00:20:39,740
So the suitors talked; but he took no heed of their words.

296
00:20:39,740 --> 00:20:42,480
He watched his father in silence — waiting, always,

297
00:20:42,480 --> 00:20:45,660
for the moment he would lay his
hands on the shameless suitors.

298
00:20:45,660 --> 00:20:49,160
And she — she had set her
beautiful chair across from them:

299
00:20:49,160 --> 00:20:54,640
Icarius' daughter, watchful-minded Penelope,
and heard each man's word in the halls.

300
00:20:54,640 --> 00:20:56,760
For laughing they had made their dinner ready —

301
00:20:56,760 --> 00:21:01,200
a sweet meal and a hearty one,
for they had slaughtered lavishly.

302
00:21:01,200 --> 00:21:06,300
But no supper could ever be more graceless than the
supper a goddess and a strong man were soon now to set

303
00:21:06,300 --> 00:21:11,232
before them. For they had begun it:
the shameful devising was theirs.
